## Work

Wrote `dashboard/__tests__/storytime-print-student-book-adversarial.test.ts`. The file holds 30
test blocks and 55 test cases. All 55 pass on c8aba128.

The split by area:

- Area 1, the HTML escape and the QR input: 16 cases in 9 blocks.
- Area 2, the layout rules of the inside pages: 9 cases in 9 blocks.
- Area 3, the CLI and the output path: 30 cases in 12 blocks.

The 5 it.each blocks give 25 plain it blocks, which matches the 25 lines that a grep for `it(`
finds. Vitest reports `storytime-print-student-book-adversarial.test.ts (55 tests)`.

### Area 1: the HTML escape and the QR input (16 cases)

- The hostile text `"><img src=x onerror=alert(1)><script>alert(2)</script>&` in 12 English
  week fields (the practice text, the formation words, the start point, the dictation word, the
  writing trace, the home words, the English home line and activity, the first song line, the
  frame, the sound, and the book title) creates no element: the element count of the whole book
  equals the element count of the clean render, and the document holds no script element, no img
  element, and no event attribute.
- The same hostile text prints as literal text on the practice page, the home front, the home
  back, and the writing page.
- A hostile start point stays inside its attribute.
- The hostile Thai text prints as text, keeps one span per run, and creates no element.
- The negative control: a broken copy with the raw payload does create an img element and a
  script element, so the escape guard is not vacuous.
- `storytimeQrUrl` rejects a full URL, a protocol-relative URL, another prefix, another case, a
  leading space, another locale, and an empty path.
- Both printed codes keep the S23 host and the `/th/st/2/1` route, and each code holds a module
  grid.
- A hostile query in the QR path stays text in the address attribute and creates no element.
- The build stops when the QR path is a full URL or a protocol-relative URL.

### Area 2: the layout rules of the inside pages (9 cases)

- The answer of a check row never prints; the control shows that the same check catches the
  answer when a broken page prints it.
- The whole book carries no answer element and no answer mark.
- Only the tear-out sides (the home front, the home back, and the six reader sides) carry a
  perforation mark.
- Every strip mark names the book, the strip number 1 to 3, and the back side; only a back side
  names the back.
- Each of the two readers has the strips 1, 2, and 3 over three leaves.
- A reader side holds only the two strip marks; the reader content waits for Phase 4.
- The two QR codes print on the home front and none on the home back.
- The aligner side holds no text, no student work, and no perforation mark.
- No attribute of any element names a color picture file; only the line-art file name prints.

### Area 3: the CLI and the output path (30 cases)

- The parser rejects the hostile `--weeks` values (a list separator, a space, an empty item, a
  leading comma, a trailing comma, a huge number, a negative number, an underscore number, an
  exponent, and a fraction) and the hostile `--version` values (0, a negative number, a fraction,
  Infinity, NaN, a value above the safe integer range, and an exponent).
- The parser rejects a flag without a value, an unknown argument, and both build modes at once.
- The parser rejects an `--out` path that is relative, a tilde path, an upper-case extension, the
  print folder, the print folder with a trailing separator, a dot-dot escape, and a non-PDF name.
- The parser accepts an `--out` path inside the print folder, including a dot-dot that stays
  inside.
- The output check rejects an output below a chain of two links that ends outside, an output
  below a relative link that points outside, an output below a relative chain, and an output that
  is a relative link to an outside file. Each test also accepts a plain inside output, so the
  check is not a rule that always throws.
- The static reading of `make-student-book-pdf.ts` shows that the output check runs before the
  folder creation, the browser launch, and the PDF/X write, and that the checked path is the
  written path.

## Findings

One NEW defect, not in the exclude list. `storytimeQrUrl` (dashboard/lib/storytime/files.ts:21)
accepts a path that leaves the `/th/st/` route, for example `/th/st/../evil`. The address then
normalises to a path outside the route, so a printed QR code can point outside the route of spec
P4. The route is Red, then Green.

Three nonblocking findings: the numeric flags accept a hex, an exponent, or a padded form; a
missing `--out` value ends in a TypeError; the query of a QR path is not checked.

